This README file describes the setup of the PDK and environment for running the sample application on
both QNX and Linux Operating systems.

1- QNX setup:
=============

	Clearing previous installation:
	======================
	Check if previous version is already present in the host PC

		sudo dpkg -l | grep "nv-driveos-build-sdk" - For sdk debian package
		sudo dpkg -l | grep "nv-driveos-qnx-qa-toolchain" - For QNX toolchain debian package

	Remove the previous sdk and qnx toolchain installations

		sudo apt remove --purge nv-driveos-build-sdk-qnx-* - For sdk debian package
		sudo apt remove --purge nv-driveos-qnx-qa-toolchain-* - For QNX toolchain debian package

	Setting up PDK folder:
	======================
	Make a directory (mkdir <top>) to copy the PDK package onto.
	cd to <top> then extract the PDK package, and then run the following commands:

		sudo -E apt -f -y --reinstall install "nv-driveos-build-sdk-qnx-*"
		(Extract all files as per installer.list to ensure dependencies are met)
		sudo -E apt -f -y --reinstall install ./nv-driveos-qnx-qa-<branch>-<GCID>_<branch>-<GCID>_amd64.deb

	Setting Environment Variables:
	==============================
	After extraction of the PDK on the host PC, execute the following instructions to set up the environment variables:

		export TOOLCHAIN_DIR_HOST=$PDK_TOP/toolchains/qnx_toolchain
		(where PDK_TOP is the directory the files from previous step are installed)
		export QNX_TOP=$PDK_TOP/drive-qnx
		export QNX_BASE=$TOOLCHAIN_DIR_HOST
		export QNX_TARGET=$QNX_BASE/target/qnx7
		export QNX_HOST=$QNX_BASE/host/linux/x86_64

	Build the sample application on the host PC:
	============================================
	The following are the instructions to build the sample application, pkcs11_reference_application:

		cd $PDK_TOP/drive-qnx/samples/nvpkcs11/external/
		make -f Makefile

	Check the build is successful and that you have the built pkcs11_reference_application.

	Unwrap reference sample:
	==============================
	The aim of the unwrap reference sample code is to demonstrate usage of the C_UnwrapKey
	API to provision a wrapped key.

	The wrapping key, used by the PKCS#11 Object Generation Tool to generate wrapped key
	objects, and the unwrapping key, used by the PKCS#11 library on the target, are both
	derived from a fuse-based key named "NV_OEM_KEY1".
	(Refer to "Derivation from Fuse-based Keys" in NVIDIA Drive OS SDK Developer Guide)

	To pass the unwrapped key section of the code, make sure the wrapped keys are generated
	and copied to the same folder as the application. The derived unwrapping key needs to have
	the same length as the key used for wrapping (i.e both 16 or both 32 bytes).

	For more details on wrapped key generation please see:
		drive-foundation/tools/security/pkcs11/keywrap/README

	In this application these files are used
		- Test_Sign_key.aad
		- Test_Sign_key.iv
		- Test_Sign_key.keytag
		- Test_Sign_key.label
		- Test_Sign_key.ctxt

	Exception: Fuse-based key unknown or board not fused
	-----------------------------------------------------
	In the event that the NV_OEM_KEY1 is not known or not fused, an independent secret key
	value can be provided.  For development purposes only, NOT to be used for production.

	To enable this, one additional file needs to be copied over.
		- Example_dev_only_key.bin

	This file should contain a binary array representing a 16 or 32 byte key value, matching
	the one used with the PKCS#11 Object Generation Tool. If this file is present, this key
	will be used instead of NV_OEM_KEY1 to derive the unwrapping key.

	Run the application on target:
	==============================
	The following are the instructions to mount and run the application on the target guest VM:

		fs-nfs3 -vv -t $HOST_IP:/$PDK_TOP/drive-qnx/samples/nvpkcs11/external/ /external
		where HOST_IP is the IP address of the host PC where the PDK is extracted

	To run the application:
		cd /external

	1. To run the application with root privileges (not suitable for production)

		./pkcs11_reference_application (or)
		iolauncher -U 0:0 ./pkcs11_reference_application

	2. Permissions needed to run the application with non-root privileges

		GIDs always required:
		# 3000 io_nvsys

		GIDs always required for CCPLEX tokens:
		# 2281 nvvse-aes0 (gpse default low)
		# 2282 nvvse-aes1 (gpse default low)
		# 2283 nvvse-sha  (gpse default low)
		# 3780 devc-nvtzvault-crypto_asym_service
		# 3790 devc-nvtzvault-crypto_sym_service

		GIDs needed as part of the sample code examples:
		# 7109 nvvse-tzse-sha1 (tzse high 1) (Only needed because TZSE_SHA_HIGH_GID channel is used in the sample code)
		# 6004 devc-nvtzvault-pkcs11_keystore_service_4 (Only needed because CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 is used in sample code)

		Additional GIDs required for non-safety build only:
		# 3500 devc-nvtzvault
		# 2280 nvvse
		# 3640 nvpkcs11

		Additional GIDs required for safety build only:
		# 45040 nvpkcs11
		# 40002 libc.so.5
		# 40006 libslog2.so.1
		# 45011 libgp.so
		# 45037 libnvos_s3_safety.so
		# 45057 libnvvse-safety.so
		# 45066 libnvdtcommon.so
		# 45071 libnvsocsys.so
		# 45112 libnvdvms_client.so

		Custom abilities required by every PKCS#11 library application:
		# nvtzvault/crypto_ability (Common for all tokens)

		Custom abilities required for CCPLEX tokens:
		# nvvse/Engines:0-2 (Allow access to GP-SE)
		# nvvse/Engines:3-5 (Allow access to TZ-SE, required for use with TZSE_SHA_HIGH_GID channel)

		Custom abilities for access to CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 token:
		# nvtzvault/pkcs11ks_dynamic_token_2_ability

		Custom abilities for NVDVMS access required for safety build only:
		# nvsys/system_info (Allow access to system information)
		# Nvdvms/ClientCommGetState (Allow access to NVDVMS framework)

		Note1:
			In order to use a token, an application must have access to both keys and engines.
			The permissions for these are handled separately. In the above example, we will use
			the keys from the CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 token, and use the GP-SE hardware engines.

			GID 6004 is to allow CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 token to be accessed. Other tokens will require
			different GIDs, these are listed in the PDK documentation.

			The library will attempt to connect to all tokens when C_Initialize is called. You will see warnings
			about failing to connect to tokens that you do not have permission to use - these can be safely ignored.

		Note2:
			When running as non-root on both safety and non-safety builds, QNX custom ability needs
			to be defined to access nvvse drivers (for hardware engine), tzvault tokens (for keys)
			and crypto TA services.

		For further details on permissions, please refer to the appropriate section in the PDK documentation.

	3. Conclusion
		To run the sample app as non-root on a safety build, using nvvse and the CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 token:

		iolauncher -U 45040:2281,2282,2283,7109,3000,3780,3790,6004,40002,40006,45011,45037,45040,45057,45066,45071,45112 \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvvse/Engines:0-5 \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvtzvault/pkcs11ks_dynamic_token_2_ability \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvtzvault/crypto_ability \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvsys/system_info \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=Nvdvms/ClientCommGetState \
			./pkcs11_reference_application

		To run the sample app as non-root on a non-safety build, using nvvse and the CCPLEX_DYNAMIC_2 token:

		iolauncher -U 3640:3000,3500,2280,2281,2282,2283,3640,3780,3790,6004,7109 \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvvse/Engines:0-5 \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvtzvault/pkcs11ks_dynamic_token_2_ability \
			-A nonroot,allow,able=nvtzvault/crypto_ability \
			./pkcs11_reference_application

	Refer to PDK documentation for more details on custom abilities

2- Linux setup:
===============

	Clearing previous installation:
	======================
	Check if previous version is already present in the host PC

		sudo dpkg -l | grep "nv-driveos-build-*" - For linux debian package

	Remove the previous sdk and qnx toolchain installations

		sudo apt remove --purge nv-driveos-build-* - For all linux debian package
		sudo apt autoremove

	Setting up the PDK folder:
	==========================
	On the host PC, make a directory (mkdir <top>) to copy the PDK package onto.
	cd to <top> then extract the PDK package, and then run the following commands:

	sudo -E apt -f -y --reinstall install "nv-driveos-build-pdk-linux-*"
	(Extract all files as per installer.list to ensure dependencies are met)

	Build the sample application on the host PC:
	============================================
	The followings are the instructions to build the sample application, pkcs11_reference_application:

		cd <top>/drive-linux/samples/nvpkcs11/external/
		make -f Makefile

	Check the build is successful and that you have the built pkcs11_reference_application.

	Run the application on target:
	==============================
	To run the application on the target guest OS (GOS), you need to copy the application as well
	as the generated wrapped keys files from the host PC onto the target.

	For more details on generation of the wrapped keys please see:
		drive-foundation/tools/security/pkcs11/keywrap/README

	scp <user>@<HOST_IP>:/<top>/drive-linux/samples/nvpkcs11/external/ /external
		Where <HOST_IP> is the IP address of the host PC.

	cd /external

	To run the application, any command that uses pkcs11 needs to have tzvault privileges.
	This can be done by adding the user temporarily to the tzvault group via sudo as follows:
		sudo -u <username to run as> -g tzvault ./pkcs11_reference_application
